<li>max: <code class="docutils literal notranslate"><span class="pre">2048</span></code></li>
<li>pattern: <code class="docutils literal notranslate"><span class="pre">arn:[\w+=/,.&#64;-]+:[\w+=/,.&#64;-]+:([\w+=/,.&#64;-]*)?:[0-9]+:[\w+=/,.&#64;-]+(:[\w+=/,.&#64;-]+)?(:[\w+=/,.&#64;-]+)?</span></code></li>
</ul>
</div></blockquote>
</div></blockquote>
</div></blockquote>
<p>Status -&gt; (string)</p>
<blockquote>
<div><p>The user pool status.</p>
<p>Possible values:</p>
<ul class="simple">
<li><code class="docutils literal notranslate"><span class="pre">Enabled</span></code></li>
<li><code class="docutils literal notranslate"><span class="pre">Disabled</span></code></li>
</ul>
</div></blockquote>
<p>LastModifiedDate -&gt; (timestamp)</p>
<blockquote>
<div>The date and time when the item was modified. Amazon Cognito returns this timestamp in UNIX epoch time format. Your SDK might render the output in a human-readable format like ISO 8601 or a Java <code class="docutils literal notranslate"><span class="pre">Date</span></code> object.</div></blockquote>
<p>CreationDate -&gt; (timestamp)</p>
<blockquote>
<div>The date and time when the item was created. Amazon Cognito returns this timestamp in UNIX epoch time format. Your SDK might render the output in a human-readable format like ISO 8601 or a Java <code class="docutils literal notranslate"><span class="pre">Date</span></code> object.</div></blockquote>
<p>ReplicaRegions -&gt; (list)</p>
<blockquote>
<div><p>A list of Amazon Web Services Regions where replicas of this user pool exist.</p>
<p>(string)</p>
<blockquote>
<div><p>Constraints:</p>
<ul class="simple">
<li>min: <code class="docutils literal notranslate"><span class="pre">0</span></code></li>
<li>max: <code class="docutils literal notranslate"><span class="pre">131072</span></code></li>
</ul>
</div></blockquote>
</div></blockquote>
</div></blockquote>
</div></blockquote>
<p>NextToken -&gt; (string)</p>
<blockquote>
<div><p>The identifier that Amazon Cognito returned with the previous request to this operation. When you include a pagination token in your request, Amazon Cognito returns the next set of items in the list. By use of this token, you can paginate through the full list of items.</p>
<p>Constraints:</p>
<ul class="simple">
<li>min: <code class="docutils literal notranslate"><span class="pre">1</span></code></li>
<li>pattern: <code class="docutils literal notranslate"><span class="pre">[\S]+</span></code></li>
</ul>
</div></blockquote>
</div>
</div>


